1. What is a Prototype (simple definition)
Prototype = an object from which other objects inherit properties and methods.
JavaScript uses prototype-based inheritance (NOT classical class inheritance like Java/C++).
👉 Every JavaScript object has a hidden internal property:
[[Prototype]]

You access it with:
Object.getPrototypeOf(obj)

(or historically)
obj.__proto__

✅ 2. Why prototypes exist (core purpose)
To enable:
✔ inheritance
✔ method sharing
✔ memory efficiency

Instead of copying methods into every object, JavaScript stores them once in the prototype.

✅ 3. Example WITHOUT prototype (bad)
function User(name) {
  this.name = name;
  this.sayHi = function() {
    console.log("Hi " + this.name);
  };
}


⚠ Problem: every object gets its OWN copy of sayHi.

Memory waste.

✅ 4. Example WITH prototype (correct)
function User(name) {
  this.name = name;
}

User.prototype.sayHi = function() {
  console.log("Hi " + this.name);
};


Now:
const u1 = new User("Manu");
const u2 = new User("Sam");
✔ both share ONE sayHi function

Huge optimization.
Interviewers LOVE this example.

🧩 5. How JavaScript looks for properties (Prototype Chain)
When you do:
obj.property
JS searches:
1️⃣ inside object
2️⃣ inside object's prototype
3️⃣ inside prototype's prototype
4️⃣ continues...

Until:
null

This is called:

⭐ Prototype Chain
Example
const animal = {
  eats: true
};

const dog = Object.create(animal);
console.log(dog.eats);


JS can't find eats in dog → checks animal → found.

🔥 6. IMPORTANT: Difference between
(A) prototype
(B) proto
(C) [[Prototype]]

Interviewers LOVE asking this.

⭐ prototype

✔ property of constructor function

function A(){}
A.prototype


Only functions have this.

⭐ __proto__

Getter/setter for object's prototype.

obj.__proto__


Legacy but still used.

⭐ [[Prototype]]

Internal hidden engine-level property.

You cannot access directly.

🏗 7. What happens when you use new

When you do:

const user = new User();


JS does:

create empty object {}

set object's prototype = User.prototype

call constructor with this = object

return object

Interview GOLD answer 🔥

🧬 8. Prototype Inheritance Example
const person = {
  speak() {
    console.log("speaking");
  }
};

const student = Object.create(person);

student.study = function() {
  console.log("studying");
};


student can use:

student.speak()


because of prototype chain.

⚠️ 9. SUPER IMPORTANT RULES (Interview traps)
🚨 Rule 1

Functions have .prototype

Objects DO NOT.

function A(){}
A.prototype   // yes

const obj = {}
obj.prototype // undefined

🚨 Rule 2

Arrow functions DO NOT have prototype.

const fn = () => {};
fn.prototype // undefined


VERY common interview question.

🚨 Rule 3

Changing prototype after object creation doesn't update existing objects automatically.

(Advanced interview trick)

🚨 Rule 4

Never modify built-in prototypes in production.

Bad:

Array.prototype.myFunc = function(){}

🏛 10. ES6 Classes are just PROTOTYPE SYNTAX

Interview bombshell answer:

👉 Classes in JS are syntactic sugar over prototypes

Example:

class User {
  constructor(name){
    this.name = name;
  }
  sayHi(){
    console.log(this.name);
  }
}


Internally becomes:

User.prototype.sayHi = function(){}

